          "solution": "<p>Since the denominator is positive, the integrand is continuous. Part (2) of the FTOC gives \\(A'(x)=(x-1)/(1+x^4).\\) Thus \\(A'&lt;0\\) on \\((0,1)\\) and \\(A'&gt;0\\) on \\((1,2).\\)</p>\n<p>Fix \\(x\\ne1\\) in \\([0,2].\\) By the Mean Value Theorem, \\(A(x)-A(1)=A'(c)(x-1)\\) for some \\(c\\) between \\(x\\) and \\(1.\\) If \\(x&lt;1,\\) both factors are negative. If \\(x&gt;1,\\) both are positive. Thus \\(A(x)&gt;A(1)\\) in either case, so \\(1\\) is the unique point of absolute minimum.</p>",
